  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Night Desk",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"The Night Desk\"**\n\nA quiet, dark study desk for long, focused sessions at a laptop. The page is a lamp-lit sheet of content, and everything around it (sidebar, top bar, table of contents) sinks into near-black so the text, formulas and worked solutions carry the light. Nothing on screen competes with the material: no decorative imagery, no gradients, no gamified chrome. Colour shows up only to tell you something.\n\nThe structure follows a docs reader: a subject sidebar on the left, a single readable column in the middle, and a sticky scrollspy table of contents on the right. Density is moderate. Long-form text gets generous line height, while tables, cheatsheets and quiz blocks tighten up. A light theme (\"Hell\") mirrors every token for daylight, and a dedicated print mode turns the cheatsheet into a dense two-column A4 sheet.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Dark by default (`data-theme=\"dark\"`), with a full light mirror and a separate print palette.",
      "One UI accent (Lamp Blue). Four further colours are reserved for callout meanings.",
      "Layering comes from tonal steps and 1px lines, not shadows.",
      "Inter for reading, JetBrains Mono for code; KaTeX math sits inline with the text.",
      "The printed A4 cheatsheet is a first-class output with its own type scale."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Semantic Colour Rule",
        "body": "Red, green, violet and amber always carry their meaning (trap, merke/correct, example/worked solution, info) and are never used decoratively. Blue is the UI accent and also means Definition. A new feature that needs colour uses Lamp Blue or the neutrals.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Mirror Rule",
        "body": "Every colour token has a light-theme counterpart and a print value. A new token isn't finished until all three exist.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Plain Heading Rule",
        "body": "Headings contain no formulas; the TOC is generated from them.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Print Scale Rule",
        "body": "The print cheatsheet has its own scale (body 8.2px, section titles 9px, code 7.8px). Screen sizes never leak into print.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Hairline Rule",
        "body": "Containers are separated by 1px lines and one tonal step, never by drop shadows.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Pressable Rule",
        "body": "Everything clickable sits on the hover-night fill with a strong hairline at rest. Hover adds a Lamp Blue border and a 8–14% Lamp Blue tint. Press moves it down 1px. Keyboard focus shows a 2px Lamp Blue outline with 2px offset. Transitions run 0.15s on colour and 0.08s on the press.",
        "section": "components"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** keep Lamp Blue as the single UI accent, and keep each signal colour tied to its callout meaning (The Semantic Colour Rule).",
      "**Do** separate surfaces with one tonal step plus a 1px hairline (The Hairline Rule).",
      "**Do** keep the reading column at 740px or less, with body text at 16px and 1.65 line height.",
      "**Do** give every new token a light-theme and a print value (The Mirror Rule).",
      "**Do** give every new clickable element the Pressable Rule's fill, hover tint, 1px press and focus outline."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** use red, green, violet or amber for decoration, emphasis or branding.",
      "**Don't** add drop shadows to cards or panels; the drawer lift is the only large shadow.",
      "**Don't** put formulas in headings (The Plain Heading Rule).",
      "**Don't** reintroduce gamified chrome (XP, levels, mission HUD); the docs reader replaced it on 2026-11-26.",
      "**Don't** let screen type sizes into the print cheatsheet (The Print Scale Rule)."
    ]
